values=a['aggregate_percent'];old=[9.673930026906403,17.944550735315396,2.535792357188383,2.5624112692512098]
rows=''.join(f'<tr><th>{name}</th><td>{b:.2f}%</td><td>{v:.2f}%</td><td>{(b-v)/b*100:.1f}%</td></tr>' for name,b,v in zip(['Water (kg)','Dry fuel (kg)','Fuel gas (kg/s)','Heating index (K)'],old,values))
body="""<nav><a href="../index.html">All reports</a> · <a href="../full-sequence/index.html">Full-sequence scorecard</a> · <a href="../full-sequence/history8-shared64-r0.html">208-history gallery</a></nav>
<h1>Longer history and a wider gated GRU</h1><p class="lead">Experiment 38 · rotation 0 · completed 30 October 2026</p>
<p>Eight prior state samples, a 64-unit GRU and a shared nonlinear output layer improved whole-sequence integral error for all four outputs on the same rotation-0 test histories as experiment 37. This is a combined architecture intervention, not an ablation of its individual changes.</p>
<h2>What changed</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Experiment 37 baseline</th><th>Experiment 38</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Prior state history</th><td>4 × 4 state values</td><td>8 × 4 state values</td></tr><tr><th>External inputs</th><td>9</td><td>9 (41 total input features)</td></tr><tr><th>Recurrent memory</th><td>One 32-unit GRU</td><td>One 64-unit GRU</td></tr><tr><th>Shared output processing</th><td>Direct output heads</td><td>Linear 64→64 followed by SiLU</td></tr><tr><th>Output heads</th><td>Four state proposals and three gates</td><td>Linear 64→4 and Linear 64→3, sigmoid gates</td></tr><tr><th>Parameters</th><td>5,895</td><td>25,159</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The added dense layer has <strong>no recurrent memory</strong>: there are not two GRU layers. The eight samples contain state values, not eight sets of precomputed state changes. Missing early history repeats the initial state. Water and fuel gates scale state changes; the gas gate scales its candidate output; heating is ungated. The three gates were retained.</p>
<h2>Matched test results</h2><p>The frozen split contains 1,662 training, 210 validation and 208 test histories; the test histories represent 204 configurations. Both models are evaluated on identical rotation-0 test IDs, simulation truths and times. Each configuration receives equal weight.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Output</th><th>Baseline integral error</th><th>New integral error</th><th>Relative reduction</th></tr></thead><tbody>ROWS</tbody></table>
<p>Integral error is the exact integral of the absolute piecewise-linear prediction error in physical units, divided by sequence duration and the simulation's full-sequence range. Lower is better. This is not area measured on the gallery's symlog screen. Active and quiet “error vs simulation” use a different denominator and are reported separately in the linked scorecard; small quiet-period denominators can produce large percentages.</p>
<h2>Training and model selection</h2><p>Seed 3516; AdamW learning rate 0.0003, weight decay 0.0001, gradient clipping 1, batch size 256; minimum 100 and maximum 400 epochs, validation patience 30. The stride-one future-window curriculum uses 1, 8, 16 and 32 steps beginning at epochs 1, 21, 41 and 61. The integral objective and gate BCE weight 0.1 are unchanged. Normalization is fitted only on training data with configuration balancing.</p><p>Training windows start from actual state history and detached actual-prefix memory, with gradients inside the forecast window. Evaluation initializes once at the initial state and feeds predictions back for the rest of the full sequence; it does not reset to later simulation truth.</p><p><strong>139 epochs completed; validation selected epoch 109 before test evaluation.</strong> At the selected epoch, training/validation full-rollout errors were 5.61% / 6.15%. At the final epoch they were 4.94% / 8.85%; the final epoch was not used as the selected model. The baseline completed 213 epochs and selected epoch 183.</p><img src="learning-curve.png" alt="Training and validation integral errors across 139 completed epochs"><p class="note">Mean of four range-normalized full-sequence integral errors, equal configuration weights. Saved values, straight connecting lines, no smoothing and no gate BCE in these curves. Vertical markers show curriculum changes.</p>
<h2>Spot interruptions and recovery</h2><p>Training used cloud Spot T4 workers. Six confirmed evictions occurred across Oregon and Iowa. Each completed epoch preserved model, optimizer, RNG, history and best-selection state; only an unfinished epoch could be lost. Continuations restored verified checkpoints rather than restarting learning.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Attempt</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Last completed epoch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>01 · Oregon b</td><td>Preempted</td><td>27</td></tr><tr><td>02 · Oregon b</td><td>Preempted after replay checks</td><td>27</td></tr><tr><td>03 · Oregon a</td><td>Queued alternative cancelled after Iowa won; no VM</td><td>No new epoch</td></tr><tr><td>04 · Iowa f</td><td>Preempted</td><td>88</td></tr><tr><td>05 · Iowa b</td><td>Preempted during provisioning</td><td>No new epoch</td></tr><tr><td>06 · Iowa a</td><td>Preempted</td><td>93</td></tr><tr><td>07 · Iowa c</td><td>Preempted after replay checks</td><td>93</td></tr><tr><td>08 · Iowa f</td><td>Succeeded</td><td>139</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The regional race allowed only the winning worker to train, after loser cleanup. Startup checks verified the actual T4, loss backward pass and identical next optimizer update from restored nonempty optimizer/RNG state. Probe weights were discarded. The original first-epoch replay proof was copied through continuations, not represented as a newly performed first epoch.</p><p>Final collection verified 295 objects with SHA/MD5 and pinned generations, all checkpoints 1–139, the selected-model-before-tests barrier and exact test population. Jobs, VMs and disks were removed, scoped input read grants removed and training reservations released. Private cloud, budget and raw evidence remain in the private DVC mirror; the report exposes derived results only.</p>
<h2>What this establishes—and what it does not</h2><p>The combined model improved this matched test comparison. Longer history, wider memory and the shared SiLU layer changed together, so these results cannot identify which caused the improvement. Hardware and Spot interruptions also differ; this is not a controlled hardware experiment or an independent-seed repeat. These splits have been examined historically, so this is exploratory evidence rather than pristine confirmation.</p><p>The source simulations represent simplified conical vegetation crowns (approximately 3 m crown diameter above a 2 m base, with categorical total heights 4/6/8/10 m), 1,600 weighted vegetation particles and a 0.5 m mesh. Ignition is imposed at 1,500°C for 20 seconds. There are no buildings or embers, and these results do not establish real-tree or property-scale fire validity. Gas is vegetation fuel-gas release, not combustion heat; heating is a surface-heating index in kelvin, not stored energy. No new physical ignition criterion or material model was validated by this neural-model change.</p><p>The gallery retains three histories per page, four plots per row, line toggles and the categorical tree/wind/ignition rubric. Those schematics are renderer encodings, not validated species or proportional fuel distributions. The “no change” reference uses the previous actual simulation state, unlike the autonomous model rollout. All earlier reports and galleries remain available.</p>
<footer><a href="https://github.com/davies-w/wildfire3d/tree/codex/ignition-surrogate-pilot/experiments/38_history8_shared64">Versioned source and DVC pointers</a> · <a href="../full-sequence/history8-shared64-r0-values.json">Derived per-history results</a></footer>""".replace('ROWS',rows)
